from pathlib import Path
import json
p=Path('content/networks/03-tcp/tcp-reliability.questions.json');q=json.loads(p.read_text())
q[0]['prompt']+=' Assume ordinary contiguous new data, no SYN/FIN and that the receiver already has all earlier data in this connection.'
q[0]['options'][3]['explanation']='Under the stated contiguous-new-data assumption, the next byte is 6201 regardless of the next segment’s length. Retransmissions could begin at other earlier sequence positions.'
q[1]['prompt']+=' Interpret this as a current cumulative ACK within one unwrapped sequence interval, not an old duplicate ACK or a SYN/FIN boundary.'
q[1]['workedExample']='In the stated sequence interval, ACK 3001 identifies the next expected position. Preceding data in this connection has been accepted contiguously by TCP; later ranges may also be buffered. This says neither how many total bytes have ever arrived nor whether the application consumed or stored them. Sequence wrap and old ACKs require the connection’s serial-number context.'
q[2]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. Waiting for more evidence reduces sensitivity to modest reordering. Three duplicates can still occur without actual loss, so it is a heuristic rather than proof.'
q[2]['options'][1]['explanation']='The threshold is a sender-side algorithm rule. Receivers often promptly ACK out-of-order data, but ACK loss, duplication and coalescing can change the observed pattern.'
q[2]['options'][3]['explanation']='The threshold is used for loss inference, not a requirement to increase the window by three segments. The classic duplicate-ACK definition does include an unchanged advertised-window condition.'
q[2]['workedExample']=q[2]['workedExample'].replace('Three duplicates means three later segments got through while B didn\'t: much stronger evidence of loss.','In the simple example with one ACK per arrival, three duplicates provide stronger evidence of a hole; they do not universally prove three distinct later segments or actual loss.')
q[3]['options'][1]['explanation']='When 501–1000 fills the gap, it joins the held 1001–1500 interval and the cumulative point advances to 1501. That held range no longer remains outstanding.'
q[3]['options'][2]['explanation']='Correct. The model merges adjacent received ranges and advances across their combined coverage once the gap fills.'
q[3]['workedExample']='Start with nxt = 1 and no held intervals.\n\n1. (1, 500) covers [1,501), so print `ACK 501 held []`.\n2. (1001, 500) covers [1001,1501), beyond the gap; print `ACK 501 held [1001]`.\n3. (501, 500) joins it into [501,1501), so print `ACK 1501 held []`.\n4. (1501, 500) extends coverage to 2001; print `ACK 2001 held []`.\n\nThe printed held list contains starts of merged ranges, not one entry per original packet.'
q[4]['options'][3]['explanation']='Even 102.5 + 2 × 42.5 would be 187.5 ms, not 205 ms. The specified estimator uses four times RTTVAR, producing 272.5 ms before bounds.'
q[4]['workedExample']=q[4]['workedExample'].replace('In practice Linux would also apply its 200 ms floor, which doesn\'t bite here.','A configured 200 ms floor would not change this estimate; the RFC’s recommended one-second floor would. The question explicitly omits those bounds, and this arithmetic does not reproduce every implementation’s estimator.')
q[5]['prompt']='In a simplified classic Reno model using the three-duplicate-ACK trigger, no Limited Transmit/TLP/RACK, no new data beyond the stated flight and one qualifying duplicate ACK per out-of-order arrival, which losses may require the RTO? Assume ACKs themselves are delivered. Select all that apply.'
q[5]['options'][3]['text']='The fast retransmission is itself lost, with no additional recovery mechanism or new acknowledgement progress'
q[5]['options'][3]['explanation']='Correct under the specified simplified recovery model. Repeated ACKs for the same gap do not establish a new recovery event; the RTO remains the fallback. Other algorithms can recover differently.'
q[5]['options'][4]['text']='Two adjacent segments exchange arrival order and none is lost'
q[5]['workedExample']='Under these assumptions: tail loss has no following data to generate duplicate ACKs; the three-segment flight with its first segment lost has only two later arrivals; a lost retransmission has no additional recovery mechanism. Those cases can need the timer. The bulk flight provides enough delivered later data for the threshold. Adjacent reordering without loss has no loss to repair. Real stacks may use additional mechanisms.'
q[6]['options'][0]['explanation']='Both candidate samples are below one second. Their size is not the ambiguity, and RFC 6298 does not mandate a 60-second cap: a chosen cap must be at least 60 seconds.'
q[6]['options'][1]['explanation']='There is no TCP rule requiring retransmissions to use a slower network queue. That does not explain which transmission the cumulative ACK covers.'
q[6]['options'][2]['explanation']='Correct. The cumulative ACK number alone cannot distinguish the copies. Timestamp-based measurement can remove the ambiguity under its specified echo rules.'
q[6]['workedExample']=q[6]['workedExample'].replace('With the timestamp option, the ACK echoes the timestamp of the copy that triggered it, so the sample is unambiguous.','The timestamp option permits RTT sampling when its echo rules disambiguate the measurement; it is not simply a universal echo of the latest arrival.')
q[7]['prompt']='A sender sent six contiguous 1,000-byte segments beginning at 1001 through 6001. It receives `ACK 1001 SACK 2001-4001, 5001-7001`, with exclusive right edges. Assume this report completely describes received later ranges, no reneging, and a separate loss detector has declared all uncovered sent ranges lost. Which segment ranges require retransmission?'
q[7]['workedExample']=q[7]['workedExample'].replace('Retransmit 1001 and 4001 only.','Given the explicit loss-detection and complete-report assumptions, retransmit 1001 and 4001. A SACK report alone does not prove every omitted range is lost or must be retransmitted immediately.')
q[8]['prompt']+=' Assume the RTO timer starts with that original transmission, no ACK restarts it, no probe or early retry intervenes, and no floor/cap or application timeout changes the stated waits.'
q[8]['options'][2]['explanation']='4.8 s is the next doubled timeout interval after the fourth expiry, not the elapsed time to retransmission four. The elapsed time is 0.3 + 0.6 + 1.2 + 2.4.'
q[8]['workedExample']=q[8]['workedExample'].split('This backoff, continued')[0]+'Linux’s actual abort timing depends on its configured limits and retry policy. An entirely idle connection with no outstanding data has no retransmission waiting to fail; use suitable keepalives, application heartbeats or operation deadlines when detection is required.'
q[9]['prompt']='After a retransmission of bytes 8001–9000, the first SACK block in an ACK is `8001-9001`, entirely below the cumulative ACK. What does this D-SACK report establish, and how might a sender use it?'
q[9]['options'][1]['text']='Those bytes were received more than once; the sender may use this evidence to assess spurious retransmission and adjust recovery or reordering tolerance'
q[9]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct. D-SACK reports duplicate receipt. It does not alone distinguish delayed originals from network duplication, or require unconditional congestion-window undo.'
q[9]['workedExample']='1. The first SACK block below the cumulative point is the D-SACK duplicate report defined by RFC 2883.\n2. It says the indicated data was received more than once.\n3. A delayed original plus retransmission is one cause; duplication elsewhere is another.\n4. A sender can combine this information with transmission history and its recovery algorithm to assess undo or greater reordering tolerance. The report alone does not prescribe a particular window change.'
q[10]['prompt']='In an RFC 8985 teaching scenario, segments 1–8 are cumulatively acknowledged and 9–10 are lost. SRTT is 40 ms, two segments remain outstanding, probe eligibility checks pass, no new data is available and the RTO deadline is later than the baseline PTO. Assume the probe and its ACK arrive. Which statements are correct?'
q[10]['options'][0]['text']='The baseline PTO is 2 × 40 = 80 ms from its scheduling point, after which the eligible probe retransmits the highest-sequence segment'
q[10]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct under the supplied assumptions. The full algorithm includes timer restarts, eligibility checks, delayed-ACK adjustment for one outstanding segment and an RTO deadline bound.'
q[10]['options'][1]['text']='The ACK of the probe can SACK segment 10 and provide RACK evidence for recovering segment 9, subject to its timing/reordering checks'
q[10]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct. Delivery of later-transmitted data helps infer loss, but an ACK does not universally force an immediate retransmission without the algorithm’s checks.'
q[10]['options'][2]['explanation']='No later original data follows the lost tail, so the simple three-duplicate-ACK trigger has no such evidence. Additional mechanisms or the RTO are needed; there is no universal 200 ms floor.'
q[10]['options'][3]['explanation']='An eligible probe can be sent before an RTO event. The congestion response depends on the recovery algorithm; it is not required to enter RTO recovery before probing.'
q[10]['workedExample']='1. With SRTT 40 ms and the stated two-segment flight, the baseline probe interval is 80 ms.\n2. Since no new data is available and eligibility checks pass, the probe retransmits segment 10.\n3. Its arrival elicits an ACK with a SACK block for that later range while 9 remains missing.\n4. RACK uses transmission/delivery times and its reordering window to decide when to mark 9 lost.\n5. No fixed total repair time follows: scheduling, path delay, further loss and recovery state still matter.'
q[11]['prompt']='An idealised TCP flow advances its data sequence space at 40 Gbit/s of payload, ignoring headers and control positions. Roughly how long does one 32-bit sequence-space wrap take, and which negotiated mechanism helps reject old duplicates?'
q[11]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. 2^32 × 8 / (40 × 10^9) ≈ 0.859 s. Negotiated timestamps support PAWS comparisons against maintained timestamp state under RFC 7323 rules.'
q[11]['options'][1]['explanation']='107 seconds does not follow from the specified payload rate. TIME_WAIT concerns connection closure/reuse, not sequence-space cycling within this continuing connection.'
q[11]['workedExample']='1. One sequence-space cycle is 2^32 data bytes.\n2. 40 Gbit/s of payload is 5 × 10^9 bytes/s.\n3. 2^32 / (5 × 10^9) = 0.8589934592 seconds.\n4. PAWS uses negotiated timestamps, maintained recent-timestamp state and serial-number comparisons to reject stale segments. It includes rules for timestamp wrap, idle state and validation; it is not “reject anything older than any timestamp ever seen”.'
p.write_text(json.dumps(q,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
